<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<!--
  DKSI Revit Suite - one installer chaining both products, for office-wide deployment.

  WHAT IT CHAINS, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE A BUNDLE RATHER THAN ONE MSI
    A single MSI has ONE install scope. These two products do not share one:

      DKSI Revit Tools (all users)   per-machine, C:\Program Files\Autodesk\Revit\Addins\2027\Cda
      Painted Material Takeoff       per-machine, C:\Program Files\Autodesk\Revit\Addins\2027\PaintedMaterialTakeoff

    They happen to agree today because the all-users variant exists. They are still two
    products with two UpgradeCodes, two uninstall entries and two release cadences, and merging
    them into one MSI would mean neither could ever ship on its own again. Burn chains them and
    leaves both independently serviceable, which is what "suite" should mean.

  ADMINISTRATIVE PRIVILEGES ARE CHECKED HERE, BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY A TEST WE WROTE.
    Both chained packages are per-machine, so Burn elevates once at the start and runs both
    inside that elevation. There is no need for - and no value in - a hand-rolled "are you an
    admin?" condition: Windows already refuses, and a custom check can only duplicate that
    answer or contradict it. A non-admin double-clicking this gets the standard UAC prompt.

  SILENT INSTALL FOR IT
        DKSI-Revit-Suite.exe /quiet /norestart /log C:\Temp\dksi.log

    /VERYSILENT IS AN INNO SETUP SWITCH AND DOES NOT EXIST HERE. Burn accepts /quiet (no UI at
    all) and /passive (progress bar, no prompts); the chained MSIs accept msiexec's /qn if run
    directly. This project has been on WiX since the first installer, and moving to Inno Setup
    to gain that one switch would trade an MSI - which GPO Software Installation, SCCM and
    Intune all consume natively - for an EXE that none of them do. The switch is not worth the
    deployment channel.

  WHAT IS NOT BUNDLED, DELIBERATELY
    THE .NET RUNTIME. Revit 2027 runs on .NET 10 and ships it as its own prerequisite, so any
    machine that can run Revit 2027 already has the runtime this add-in needs. Chaining a
    redistributable would add ~55 MB to every deployment to install something that is provably
    already present, and would fail the "clean corporate workstation" test in the other
    direction by trying to install a runtime IT may manage centrally.

    THE REVIT API ASSEMBLIES. RevitAPI.dll and RevitAPIUI.dll are referenced with Private=false
    and must NEVER be shipped - a private copy causes type-identity mismatches at runtime, the
    ones that read as "cannot cast Element to Element". They come from the Revit install.

    THE SIGNING CERTIFICATE for Painted Material Takeoff. Trusting a self-signed root is a real
    change to a workstation's security posture and is not a decision an installer should make
    silently on anyone's behalf. It stays a separate, documented, deliberate step - see
    "installer/PaintTakeoff/Trust-Certificate.ps1". If this suite is going past a handful of
    machines, the right fix is an organisational code-signing certificate, after which the step
    disappears entirely.

  SO WHAT IS BUNDLED IS EVERYTHING THIS SUITE ACTUALLY OWNS: both add-in assemblies, both
  manifests, the shared parameter file, the time-tracking defaults, and the embedded icons that
  are compiled into the DLL itself. There are no NuGet dependencies in either project - the
  DKSI assembly has zero PackageReference entries - so there is nothing else to carry.
-->

<Wix xmlns="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs"
     xmlns:bal="http://wixtoolset.org/schemas/v4/wxs/bal">

  <Bundle
      Name="DKSI Revit Suite"
      Manufacturer="DKSI"
      Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
      UpgradeCode="9a5f27e3-6c81-4d40-bb92-3e074f1c8a56"
      Compressed="yes"
      DisableModify="yes">

    <!--
      NOT Theme="none". THAT WAS A BUG AND IT COST A FAILED ROLLOUT.

      "none" does not mean "a minimal UI". It means NO WINDOW AT ALL: wixstdba renders nothing,
      so double-clicking the bundle raises a UAC prompt and then, from the user's side, does
      nothing whatsoever. It installs correctly and silently and looks exactly like an installer
      that failed to start. The first person who ran it reported it "didn't run"; it had almost
      certainly installed.

      "none" is for bundles that are ONLY ever launched with /quiet by a deployment system. This
      one is also handed to people, so it needs a window.

      rtfLicense shows the same Notice.rtf the per-user MSI already shows, so both installers
      say the same thing to the same audience. The interactive click-through it adds costs
      nothing in deployment: /quiet suppresses the UI entirely, whatever the theme.
    -->
    <BootstrapperApplication>
      <bal:WixStandardBootstrapperApplication
          Theme="rtfLicense"
          LicenseFile="$(var.NoticeRtf)"
          SuppressOptionsUI="yes" />
    </BootstrapperApplication>

    <!--
      ORDER MATTERS. DKSI Revit Tools installs first because Painted Material Takeoff 1.0.3 no
      longer builds a ribbon of its own - it relies on the DKSI ribbon to surface its three
      tools. Installing them the other way round works, but leaves a window in which the takeoff
      is installed and unreachable, which is exactly the state that generated a support question
      once already.

      Both are per-machine, so the bundle elevates once and neither prompts again.
    -->
    <Chain>
      <!-- Neither shows its own MSI UI: suppressed is the default from WiX v4 on, and a
           chained package that opened its own wizard would break the bundle's silent mode. -->
      <MsiPackage
          Id="DksiRevitTools"
          SourceFile="$(var.DksiMsi)"
          Vital="yes" />

      <MsiPackage
          Id="PaintedMaterialTakeoff"
          SourceFile="$(var.PaintTakeoffMsi)"
          Vital="no" />
    </Chain>

    <!--
      Vital="no" on the takeoff is deliberate. DKSI Revit Tools is useful on its own and is the
      thing most of the office needs; the takeoff is a separate product with its own signing
      story. If it fails - and the most likely reason is a machine whose policy blocks the
      unsigned MSI - the suite should still leave a working DKSI ribbon behind rather than roll
      the whole deployment back. The three takeoff buttons are omitted from that ribbon
      automatically when the assembly is not on disk, so a partial install degrades cleanly
      instead of leaving dead buttons.
    -->

  </Bundle>

</Wix>
